BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the syntax of linking \(copular\) verbs and explain the selection of predicate adjectives versus adverbs.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Nature of Copular Verbs:) Tj
T*
(   Linking verbs connect the subject noun phrase to a subject complement \(predicate noun or) Tj
T*
(   predicate adjective\) rather than expressing dynamic physical actions:) Tj
T*
(   - True Linking Verbs: be, become, seem.) Tj
T*
(   - Sensory / State Linking Verbs: appear, look, smell, taste, sound, feel, remain, stay, turn.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Predicate Adjective Rule:) Tj
T*
(   Because the complement describes the subject noun, it must be an adjective, not an adverb:) Tj
T*
(   - Incorrect: "The freshly prepared buffer smelled pungently.") Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "The freshly prepared buffer smelled pungent.") Tj
T*
(   - Incorrect: "The bacterial suspension appeared turbidly under illumination.") Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "The bacterial suspension appeared turbid under illumination.") Tj
T*
(   - Incorrect: "The sample remained unstably at room temperature.") Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "The sample remained unstable at room temperature.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Contrast with Action Verbs:) Tj
T*
(   When sensory verbs denote physical action rather than a state, an adverb is required:) Tj
T*
(   - Copular: "The spectrophotometer looks accurate." \(Adjective describing the instrument\).) Tj
T*
(   - Dynamic: "The spectrophotometer measured the absorbance accurately." \(Adverb modifying the) Tj
T*
(   verb measured\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 12:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the rules governing degrees of comparison in adjectives and highlight errors in technical data descriptions.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Morphological Comparison Rules:) Tj
T*
(   - One-Syllable Adjectives: Form comparative with -er and superlative with -est \(pure -> purer) Tj
T*
(   -> purest; dense -> denser -> densest\).) Tj
T*
(   - Multi-Syllable Adjectives: Use analytic periphrasis with 'more' and 'most' \(viscous -> more) Tj
T*
(   viscous -> most viscous; sensitive -> more sensitive -> most sensitive\).) Tj
T*
(   - Two-Syllable Adjectives: Vary by ending \(-y takes -ier: heavy -> heavier; -ous, -ful, -ed) Tj
T*
(   take more: sterile -> more sterile\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Critical Comparison Errors in Scientific Writing:) Tj
T*
(   - Double Comparatives: Strictly ungrammatical \(*more higher, *more denser\).) Tj
T*
(   - Correction: "The yield was significantly higher" \(Use adverbs of degree like significantly,) Tj
T*
(   substantially, slightly\).) Tj
T*
(   - Incomplete Comparisons: Comparisons must state the benchmark of contrast:) Tj
T*
(   - Incomplete: "The mutant strain grew faster.") Tj
T*
(   - Complete: "The mutant strain grew faster than the wild-type control strain.") Tj
T*
(   - Illogical Comparisons \(Apples-to-Oranges\):) Tj
T*
(   - Illogical: "The molecular weight of protein A is greater than protein B.") Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "The molecular weight of protein A is greater than that of protein B.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 13:) Tj
T*
ET
