ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the syntax and mechanics of the split infinitive in formal scientific and biomedical prose.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Morphological Nature of the Infinitive:) Tj
T*
(   The full infinitive consists of the particle 'to' followed by the base lexical verb form \(e.g.,) Tj
T*
(   to purify, to amplify, to titrate\). A 'split infinitive' occurs when an adverb or adverbial) Tj
T*
(   phrase is inserted between 'to' and the verb stem:) Tj
T*
(   - Example: "to thoroughly vortex the sample.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Historical Prescriptive View vs. Contemporary Reality:) Tj
T*
(   - 19th-century grammarians condemned split infinitives by analogy with Latin \(where the) Tj
T*
(   infinitive is a single indivisible word, e.g., purificare\).) Tj
T*
(   - Modern Linguistics: Splitting an infinitive is completely grammatical in Germanic syntax and) Tj
T*
(   often improves clarity and natural emphasis.) Tj
T*
(   - Stylistic Recommendation in Research:) Tj
T*
(   - Avoid awkward splits: "We intended to rapidly and without error sequence the clone" -> "We) Tj
T*
(   intended to sequence the clone rapidly and without error.") Tj
T*
(   - Retain splits if unsplitting creates syntactic clumsiness or ambiguity: "The goal is to) Tj
T*
(   better understand enzyme kinetics" is clearer than "The goal is better to understand...") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 27:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Discuss the proper syntactic deployment of Latin abbreviations \(i.e., e.g., et al., etc., vs.\) in academic publications.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Taxonomic Inventory and Meanings:) Tj
T*
(   - i.e. \(id est\): Meaning 'that is' or 'in other words'. Used to specify or clarify an exact) Tj
T*
(   definition:) Tj
T*
(   - "The wild-type strain is prototrophic \(i.e., capable of synthesizing all essential) Tj
T*
(   nutrients\).") Tj
T*
(   - e.g. \(exempli gratia\): Meaning 'for example'. Introduces non-exhaustive illustrative) Tj
T*
(   examples:) Tj
T*
(   - "Model eukaryotic organisms \(e.g., Saccharomyces cerevisiae, Caenorhabditis elegans\) were) Tj
T*
(   studied.") Tj
T*
(   - et al. \(et alii / et aliae\): Meaning 'and others'. Note: 'et' has no period; 'al.' has a) Tj
T*
(   period:) Tj
T*
(   - "Watson et al. \(1953\) elucidated DNA double-helix architecture.") Tj
T*
(   - etc. \(et cetera\): Meaning 'and other things'. Strictly discouraged in rigorous scientific) Tj
T*
(   papers; prefer 'and colleagues' or explicit lists.) Tj
T*
(   - vs. / v. \(versus\): Meaning 'against' or 'in contrast to'.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Punctuation Convention:) Tj
T*
(   In American scientific English, both 'i.e.' and 'e.g.' must be enclosed in parentheses and) Tj
T*
(   followed by a comma: "\(i.e., ...\)" and "\(e.g., ...\)".) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
